familiar with the case . A <ei26 seizure> also would <ei27 make> the case the largest -- and one of the first -- in which lawyers ' fees have been confiscated in a prosecution unrelated to drugs . \" The people who suffer in the short run are defendants , but the people who suffer in the long run are all of the people , because there wo n't be a vigorous private bar to defend the Bill of Rights , \" <ei29 says> Gerald Lefcourt , a criminal defense attorney who <ei30 says> he has <ei31 turned> down a number of cases to <ei32 avoid> possible fee <ei33 seizures> . Mr. Antar is being <ei34 investigated> by a federal grand jury in Newark , where prosecutors have <ei35 told> him that they may soon <ei37 seek> an indictment on racketeering and securities fraud charges . Under the Racketeer Influenced and Corrupt Organizations law , or RICO , the government <ei38 has> the authority to <ei39 seek> to <ei40 freeze> or <ei41 seize> a defendant 's assets before <ei42 trial> . <ei43 According> to individuals familiar with Mr. Antar 's case , prosecutors <ei44 issued> their warning this week after one of Mr. Antar 's attorneys <ei46 asked> whether legal fees might be <ei47 subject> to <ei48 seizure> . In a letter , prosecutors <ei49 told> Mr. Antar 's lawyers that because of the recent Supreme Court <ei52 rulings> , they could <ei53 expect> that any fees collected from Mr. Antar may be <ei54 seized> . Prosecutors have <ei55 told> Mr. Antar 's attorneys that they <ei56 believe> Mr. Antar 's allegedly ill-gotten gains are so great that any money he has used to pay attorneys <ei57 derives> from illegal <ei58 activities> . Therefore , they <ei60 said> , the money can be <ei61 taken> from the lawyers even after they are <ei62 paid> . Justin Feldman and Jack Arseneault , attorneys for Mr. Antar , both <ei63 declined> to <ei64 comment> on the matter . In Newark , U.S. Attorney Samuel A. Alito <ei65 said> , \" I do n't <ei66 think> there 's any legal reason to <ei67 limit> forfeiture of 